1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,996
Chapter 3: Millions of Books

2
00:00:03,346 --> 00:00:09,115
Still, how could an 1893 book get so much right? Let's go to the chalkboard.

3
00:00:09,565 --> 00:00:19,411
A quick question. In a room of 23 people, how likely is it that two share the same birthday? It sounds tiny. It's over 50 percent.

4
00:00:19,611 --> 00:00:26,842
Because you aren't comparing one person to another. You compare everyone to everyone. That's 253 possible pairs.

5
00:00:27,042 --> 00:00:35,350
Now think about all the books of the 19th century. Thousands of characters with unusual names. And millions of people hunting for matches.

6
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:45,242
The same thing happens with The Simpsons. In a 2000 episode, Lisa becomes president and inherits the mess of a President Trump. It was a joke about something absurd.

7
00:00:45,442 --> 00:00:53,134
With so many combinations, the strange thing would be finding none. And if you draw the bullseye after shooting the arrow, you never miss.

8
00:00:54,084 --> 00:00:57,238
Out of millions of books, one of them had to hit.
